    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d748">

                <pb facs="1031" n="905" xml:id="pg905"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.74/552: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Mayer</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-04-28T19:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 28,
                                1925—7 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>87. Paragraph 1 your 168, April 23, 1 P.M. You may inform Foreign Office that
                    there are no negotiations pending with the Chinese Minister here. In handing to
                    him the memoranda<anchor n="15" xml:id="d748-f1"/> referred to in the
                    Department’s 75 April 11, noon,<anchor n="16" xml:id="d748-f2"/> he was orally
                    requested to give his support to the proposal but it is understood that he has
                    taken no action in this direction.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d748-f1" n="15" xml:id="d748-f1-content">
                <p>Memoranda to the Japanese Embassy and to the Chinese Legation, dated April 8,
